# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the course as one story (final, course review)
STORY = [
    ("1 · Scarcity and choice (Ch 1)", "Everything starts with scarcity: we choose, trade off and respond to incentives. Macroeconomics asks those questions for the whole economy."),
    ("2 · Measuring the economy (Ch 4–5)", "GDP measures output (production = expenditure = income); real GDP strips out prices. The unemployment rate and the CPI measure the other two big indicators."),
    ("3 · The long run (Ch 6–7)", "Potential GDP grows with labor and, above all, productivity. Productivity needs capital, and capital is financed by saving through the loanable funds market."),
    ("4 · Money and the world (Ch 8–9)", "Banks create money; the Fed controls the monetary base. In the long run, money growth sets inflation. Interest rates and expectations set exchange rates, and net exports link us to the world."),
    ("5 · The short run (Ch 10–12)", "AS–AD explains fluctuations: AD and SAS shift, real GDP moves away from potential, and money wages slowly bring it back. The multiplier explains why AD moves so much; inflation is demand-pull or cost-push; the Phillips curve shows the short-run tradeoff."),
    ("6 · Policy (Ch 13–14)", "Fiscal policy (G, T) and monetary policy (the interest rate) shift AD to close gaps. Both have lags; in the long run they can't keep real GDP above potential, only change the price level."),
]

